# The login picture (vtium/vtium#63, ADR-1100 D17): a picture vtium.conf
# names with login-image, placed each time a login prompt starts, as a
# background beneath the text or as a banner above the prompt.
#
# The development VT's vtium needs a build with images and these lines in
# the configuration it reads, before the run starts:
#
#   login-image=/usr/share/pixmaps/archlinux-logo.png
#   login-image-fit=cover
#
# Any PNG or JPEG file root can read will do. The guide runs as the
# session of a console that started with the picture.
#
#   make vt-check GUIDE=login

title: the login picture at the login prompt

step: The picture behind the login prompt
do: look at the whole screen before you press anything.
expect: the picture *fills the screen* behind the text, keeping its shape,
expect: with no band at any edge but a strip *less than one character*
expect: wide at the right or the bottom. Every line of text reads cleanly
expect: over it.

step: Colored cells cover the picture
run: printf '\033[44m blue cells \033[0m plain text \033[7m reverse \033[0m\n'
do: read the line the command printed.
expect: the blue cells and the reversed word are *solid*, with no picture
expect: showing through them, and the plain text draws over the picture.

step: A screen clear removes it
run: clear
do: look at the whole screen.
expect: the picture is *gone*, with the default background everywhere, and
expect: the prompt at the top.

step: A new console gets its own picture
do: open a new console with the open chord, look at its login prompt, then
do: close it and come back here.
expect: the new console's login prompt shows the picture behind it
expect: *at once*, as the first step did.

step: A program cannot name it
do: open a new console with the open chord and log in. At the shell type
do: printf '\033_Ga=d,d=i,i=1\033\\' then Enter, then printf
do: '\033_Ga=d,d=z,z=-2147483648\033\\' then Enter. Then close it.
expect: the first command *leaves the picture* as it was, since the picture
expect: has no image id; the second, a delete by its depth, *removes it*.
